Consider the linear system dynamics with scalar input
\begin{equation}\label{s1:sys}
\dot x(t) = A_p x(t) + b u(t)
\end{equation}
where ${x(t) \in \mathbb R^n}$ is the state vector, ${u(t)\in \mathbb R}$ is the control input, ${A_p\in \mathbb R^{n \times n}}$ is unknown and ${b\in \mathbb R^n}$ is known.

Our goal is to design the control input such that ${x(t)}$ follows the reference model state ${x_m(t)\in \mathbb R^n}$ defined by  the following dynamics
\begin{equation}\label{s1:ref}
\dot x_m(t) = A_m x_m(t) + b r(t) - L(x(t)-x_m(t))
\end{equation}
where ${A_m \in \mathbb R^{n\times n }}$ is Hurwtitz and ${r(t)\in \mathbb R}$ is a bounded possible time varying reference command. ${L\in \mathbb R^{n\times n}}$ is denoted as the {\em Luenberger--gain}, and is chosen such that 
\begin{equation}\label{s1:ambar}
\bar A_m \triangleq A_m+L
\end{equation}
is Hurwitz.

\begin{equation}\label{s1:ref}
\dot x_m(t) = A_m x_m(t) + b r(t) - L(x(t)-x_m(t))
\end{equation}

\label{asm:m}
A parameter vector ${\theta^*\in\mathbb R^n}$ exists that satisfies the {\em matching condition}
\begin{equation}\label{s1:match}
A_m=A_p+b\theta^{*T}.
\end{equation}

The control input is chosen in the form
\begin{equation}\label{s1:u}
u(t)= \theta^T(t) x(t) + r(t)
\end{equation}
where $\theta(t) \in \mathbb R^n$ is the adaptive control gain with the update law
\begin{equation}\label{s1:law}
\dot \theta(t) = -\Gamma x(t)e^T(t) Pb
\end{equation}
with ${\Gamma = \Gamma^T>0}$, ${e(t)= x(t)-x_m(t)}$ is the model following error and $P=P^T>0$ is the solution to the algebraic Lyapunov equation
\begin{equation}
\label{s1:lyap}\bar A_m^TP+P \bar A_m = -I_{n\times n}. 
\end{equation}

 It is straight forward to show using \eqref{s1:law} and \eqref{s1:et} that
\begin{equation}\label{can}
V(e,\tilde\theta) = e^TPe + \tilde \theta^T \Gamma^{-1} \tilde \theta
\end{equation}
is a Lyapunov function. Since $e$ is bounded, the structure of \eqref{s1:ref} implies that $x_m$ is bounded. $x$ in turn and $u$ are bounded. Barbalat  lemma ensures asymptotic convergence of $e(t)$ to zero.

The standard open-loop reference model is given by 
\begin{equation}\dot x_m^o(t) = A_m x_m^o(t) + b r(t) \label{eo}\end{equation}
with the corresponding tracking error 
\begin{equation} e^o(t) =  x(t) - x_m^o(t) \label{eodef}. \end{equation}

