Any Hurwitz matrix ${A_m\in \mathbb R^{n\times n}}$ with constants $a$ and $\sigma$ as defined in \eqref{s1:ambound} satisfies the following bound for the matrix exponential  
\begin{equation*}
\norm {\exp(A_m\tau)} \leq m_\delta \exp((-\sigma+\delta a) \tau)
\end{equation*}
where 
$m_\delta= \frac{3}{2}\left(1+\frac 2 \delta\right)^{n-1}$ and $\delta>0$. The proof follows directly from \cite{sol94}.

Setting $\delta = \sigma/(2a)$ the following holds
\begin{equation}\label{exp2}
\norm {\exp(A_m \tau)} \leq m \exp\left(-\frac \sigma 2 \tau\right),
\end{equation}
where $m =\frac{3}{2} \left(1+4 \varkappa\right)^{n-1}$ and ${\varkappa = \frac a \sigma}$.

For any diagonal matrix ${L=-lI_{n\times n}}$ the following bound holds for the matrix exponential
\begin{equation}\label{exp3}
\norm {\exp(L \tau )}  \leq  \exp(- l  \tau) 
\end{equation}
The proof follows from \cite[Section 2]{loa77}.

Beginning with the integral form of Lyapunov's equation in \eqref{s1:lyap}
${P=\int_0^\infty\exp(\bar A_m^T \tau)\exp(\bar A_m \tau)\ d\tau}.$ Due to our choice of $L$, $A_m$ and $L$ commute, thus ${\exp(A_m+L)=\exp(A_m)\exp(L)}$ and
\begin{equation*} \label{app3:p}
P =   \int_0^\infty \exp(A_m^T\tau) \exp(L^T\tau)  \exp(A_m \tau)\exp(L \tau)\ d\tau.
\end{equation*}
Using the bound in \eqref{exp2} and \eqref{exp3} the integral just above can be upper bounded and the bound in \eqref{s1:pbound} directly follows.

Let $\xi\in\mathbb R^n$ be a normalized eigenvector of $P$. By pre-- and postmultiplying \eqref{s1:lyap} by $\xi^T$ and $\xi$, we have 
\begin{equation*}
\xi^T \bar A_m^TP \xi + \xi^T P\bar A_m \xi =- \xi^TI_{n\times n} \xi
\end{equation*}
which reduces to 
\begin{equation*}
 \lambda_i (P) \xi^T (\bar A_m +\bar A_m^T)\xi =-1.
\end{equation*}
Expanding $\bar A_m$ we have
\begin{equation*}
 \lambda_i (P) \xi^T ( A_m + A_m^T-2 l I_{n\times n }) \xi =-1.
\end{equation*}
Finally, using the definitions in \eqref{s1:ambound} and taking the minimum eigenvalue of $P$ we arrive at \eqref{minP} \cite{mor84}.

